1
00:04:27,692 --> 00:04:33,692
The most terrifying scream is always the last.

2
00:08:55,384 --> 00:09:01,384
Scream 3 is a horror, mystery film directed by Wes Craven.

3
00:13:23,076 --> 00:13:29,076
As bodies begin dropping around the Hollywood set of STAB 3, the third film based on the gruesome Woodsboro
killings, Sidney and other survivors are once again terrorized by another Ghostface killer.

4
00:17:50,769 --> 00:17:56,769
Dewey Riley, played by David Arquette.

5
00:22:18,461 --> 00:22:24,461
Sidney Prescott, played by Neve Campbell.

6
00:26:46,153 --> 00:26:52,153
Gale Weathers, played by Courteney Cox.

7
00:31:13,846 --> 00:31:19,846
Mark Kincaid, played by Patrick Dempsey.

8
00:35:41,538 --> 00:35:47,538
Roman Bridger, played by Scott Foley.

9
00:40:09,230 --> 00:40:15,230
John Milton, played by Lance Henriksen.

10
00:44:36,923 --> 00:44:42,923
Scream 3 is a 2000 American slasher film directed by
Wes Craven and written by Ehren Kruger.

11
00:49:04,615 --> 00:49:10,615
It stars David Arquette, Neve Campbell, Courteney Cox Arquette, Parker Posey, Patrick Dempsey, Scott Foley, Lance
Henriksen, Matt Keeslar, Jenny McCarthy, Emily Mortimer, Deon Richmond, and Patrick Warburton.

12
00:53:32,307 --> 00:53:38,307
It is a sequel to Scream 2 (1997) and the third
installment in the Scream film series.

13
00:58:00,000 --> 00:58:06,000
The film's story follows Sidney Prescott (Campbell), who has gone into self-imposed isolation following the events of the previous
two films but is drawn to Hollywood after a new Ghostface begins killing the cast of the film within a film Stab 3.

14
01:02:27,692 --> 01:02:33,692
Scream 3 combines the violence of the slasher genre with comedy and
"whodunit" mystery, while satirizing the cliché of film trilogies.

15
01:06:55,384 --> 01:07:01,384
Unlike the previous Scream films, there was an increased emphasis on comedic elements in this installment; the violence and horror were
reduced in response to increased public scrutiny about violence in media, following the Columbine High School massacre.

16
01:11:23,076 --> 01:11:29,076
Scream (1996) screenwriter Kevin Williamson provided a five-page outline for two sequels to Scream when
auctioning his original script, hoping to entice bidders with the potential of buying a franchise.

17
01:15:50,769 --> 01:15:56,769
Williamson's commitments to other projects meant he was unable to develop a complete script for Scream 3,
so writing duties were undertaken by Kruger, who discarded many of Williamson's notes.

18
01:20:18,461 --> 01:20:24,461
Craven and Marco Beltrami returned to direct and score the film, respectively.

19
01:24:46,153 --> 01:24:52,153
Production was troubled, including script rewrites, occasions when pages were only ready
on the day of filming, and scheduling difficulties with the main cast.

20
01:29:13,846 --> 01:29:19,846
Principal photography took place from July to September 1999,
and the ending was re-filmed in January 2000.

21
01:33:41,538 --> 01:33:47,538
Scream 3 premiered on February 3, 2000, in Westwood, Los Angeles, and was theatrically released
the following day, grossing $161.8 million worldwide on a budget of $40 million.

22
01:38:09,230 --> 01:38:15,230
The film received mixed reviews but has been reappraised in
recent years in the wake of the MeToo movement.

23
01:42:36,923 --> 01:42:42,923
Scream 3 was originally intended to be the final installment of the series until the
franchise was revived with a sequel, Scream 4, released on April 15, 2011.

24
01:47:04,615 --> 01:47:10,615
The film explores themes of mask, isolation, movie business, ex-cop, sequel.

25
01:51:32,307 --> 01:51:38,307
The tone is dark and mind_bending.
